(Every instruction shape of the corpus, encoded and compared with nasm.

 The shapes table, tests/toolchain/shapes.tsv, holds one row for each
 mnemonic and operand shape the compiler's output and coreasm use, with an
 example line and the bytes nasm -f bin gives it at 0x401000, its labels at
 0x500000 and every eight bytes on. This test builds the record of each
 example, encodes it at the same address with the labels in the same places,
 and compares the bytes. It also checks that the size the encoder measures
 is the size it lays down, and, for a line that names a label, that its
 bytes agree with the object-file bytes nasm gives outside the field the
 linker fills in.

 The jumps are the one place vas differs from nasm on purpose: nasm encodes
 a jump to a target in reach in two bytes, and vas always takes rel32. The
 table's example jumps to a target out of reach, so their bytes are nasm's
 rel32 form; the test names those rows.

 usage: shapes_test <path of shapes.tsv>
 Without a path it reads tests/toolchain/shapes.tsv from the directory it
 runs in. It exits 1 if any row differs, or if it cannot read the table.)

see "./records.vox".
see "../../../../tools/vas/encoder.vox".

a text called 'table path' is "tests/toolchain/shapes.tsv".
If arguments's count is greater than 1 then, set 'table path' to arguments's first.

a list called 'encoding table' is [].
a list called 'row mnemonics' is [].
a text called 'table problems' is 'prepare the encoder' with 'encoding table' and 'row mnemonics'.
If 'table problems' is not "" then,
  Print "The encoding table has problems:\n{table problems}",
  Exit 1.

a map called registers is 'the register map'.
a buffer called scratch.

(The whole shapes table, one text to a line.)
To 'the lines of the file' of a text called path.
  a list called lines is [].
  a buffer called content.
  open a file for reading called source at path.
  On error Return a list, lines.
  Create a buffer called chunk.
  Read from source into chunk.
  While chunk's size is greater than 0,
    append chunk to content,
    Read from source into chunk.
  Close source.
  a buffer called line.
  For each position from 1 to content's size,
    a number called character is byte position of content,
    if character is 'the line break' then, 'keep the line' with line and lines.
    if character is not 'the line break' then, 'add the byte to' with character and line..
  'keep the line' with line and lines.
  Return a list, lines.

To 'keep the line' with a buffer called line and a list called lines.
  If line's size is 0 then, Return.
  a text called 'finished line' is line as text.
  append 'finished line' to lines.
  clear line.

(Whether the bytes agree with an object-file form such as
 "48 8d 35 [00 00 00 00]": the same number of bytes, and the same bytes
 outside the brackets, which mark the field the linker fills in.)
To 'the bytes agree with the object form' with a buffer called encoded and a text called 'object form'.
  a list called pieces is 'the pieces of the row' with 'object form' and ' ' and false.
  If pieces's length is not encoded's size then, Return a boolean, false.
  a boolean called 'it is relocated' is false.
  For each 'byte number' from 1 to pieces's length,
    a text called piece is element 'byte number' of pieces,
    a buffer called characters is "{piece}",
    if byte 1 of characters is '[' then, set 'it is relocated' to true.
    a text called digits is 'the hex digits of the piece' of piece,
    a number called 'object byte' is digits as a hex number,
    if 'it is relocated' is false and 'object byte' is not byte 'byte number' of encoded then, Return a boolean, false.
    if byte {characters's size} of characters is ']' then, set 'it is relocated' to false..
  Return a boolean, true.

(A piece of an object form without its brackets.)
To 'the hex digits of the piece' of a text called piece.
  a buffer called characters is "{piece}".
  a buffer called digits.
  For each position from 1 to characters's size,
    a number called character is byte position of characters,
    if character is not '[' and character is not ']' then, 'add the byte to' with character and digits..
  Return a text, digits as text.

a list called rows is 'the lines of the file' of 'table path'.
If rows's length is 0 then,
  Print "shapes_test: cannot read the shapes table at {table path}",
  Exit 1.

a number called 'rows read' is 0.
a number called 'rows exact' is 0.
a number called 'object forms checked' is 0.
a number called 'object forms agreeing' is 0.
a list called 'rel32 rows' is [].
a list called 'differing rows' is [].

(The first line names the columns: mnemonic, operands, count, files,
 example, source, bytes, object bytes and flags.)
For each 'row number' from 2 to rows's length,
  a text called row is element 'row number' of rows,
  a list called fields is 'the pieces of the row' with row and {'the tab'} and true,
  a text called example is element 5 of fields,
  a text called expected is element 7 of fields,
  a text called 'object form' is element 8 of fields,
  a text called flags is element 9 of fields,
  increment 'rows read',
  a map called labels is {},
  a map called record is 'the record of the line' with example and labels and registers,
  a text called problem is record's "problem",
  a buffer called output,
  if problem is "" then, set problem to 'encode the instruction' with record and 0x401000 and 'encoding table' and 'row mnemonics' and output.
  a text called encoded is 'the bytes in hex' of output,
  a number called size is 'the size of the instruction' with record and 'encoding table' and 'row mnemonics' and scratch,
  a boolean called 'it is exact' is false,
  if problem is "" and encoded is expected and size is output's size then, set 'it is exact' to true.
  if 'it is exact' then, increment 'rows exact'.
  if 'it is exact' is false then, append "{example}: nasm gives {expected}; vas gives {encoded}, measures {size} bytes; {problem}" to 'differing rows'.
  if 'the text begins with' with flags and "rel8:" then, append example to 'rel32 rows'.
  if 'object form' is not "-" then, increment 'object forms checked'.
  if 'object form' is not "-" and {'the bytes agree with the object form' with output and 'object form'} then, increment 'object forms agreeing'..

To 'the text begins with' with a text called 'whole text' and a text called beginning.
  a buffer called whole is "{whole text}".
  a buffer called start is "{beginning}".
  If start's size is greater than whole's size then, Return a boolean, false.
  For each position from 1 to start's size,
    if byte position of whole is not byte position of start then, Return a boolean, false..
  Return a boolean, true.

For each difference in 'differing rows',
  Print "DIFFERS: {difference}".
Print "{rows exact} of {rows read} shapes encode exactly as nasm -f bin encodes them.".
Print "{object forms agreeing} of {object forms checked} lines that name a label agree with nasm's object-file bytes outside the relocated field.".
Print "{'rel32 rows''s length} rows are jumps nasm would shorten to rel8 for a target in reach; vas always encodes rel32, so they are compared with nasm's rel32 form for a target out of reach:".
For each jump in 'rel32 rows',
  Print "  {jump}".
If 'rows exact' is not 'rows read' or 'object forms agreeing' is not 'object forms checked' then, Exit 1.
